Anarchy can be said to “work better than you think” if the mechanisms of self-governance that undergird it work in circumstances where you thought self-governance couldn't.
This book considers several such circumstances.
Part I contains essays that address self-governance when populations are socially diverse.
Part II contains essays that address self-governance when individuals face the specter of physical violence.
Part III contains essays that address self-governance in societies composed exclusively of “bad apples” – persons whose way of living is devoted to theft and murder.